Summary: Add command-line flags so CEF-based launchers (Steam, Battle.net, EA app and others) render
Category: graphics
Kind: workaround
Origin: reconstructed
  Version: Sikarugir 10.0_8
  Binary: Sikarugir WS12WineSikarugir10.0_8 kernelbase.dll (x86_64 and i386)
  Functions: hack_append_command_line's table (x86_64 file offset 0x9c1a0)
  Covers: the table's seven entries and their flags; EADesktop, the three
    GOG Galaxy entries and the SocialClubHelper flags are Sikarugir's own
  Changes: matches the program's file name, in any case (10.0_8 looks for
    the name anywhere in the path, case-sensitively)
Origin: crossover (ported here)
  Version: 24.0.7
  CW-Hacks: 13322, 17315, 21883, 19610
  URL: https://media.codeweavers.com/pub/crossover/source/crossover-sources-24.0.7.tar.gz
  Files: dlls/kernelbase/process.c
  Covers: the mechanism (hack_append_command_line and its call) and the
    steamwebhelper and Battle.net entries
  Changes: the function and its call; steamwebhelper and Battle.net entries
    as in CX. CX's other 15 entries left out (10.0_8 has none of them).
    Matches the file name with wcsicmp (CX: wcsstr on the path). The new
    command line is sized from tidy_cmdline and checked for failure (CX
    sizes it from cmd_line, which overflows when cmd_line is NULL or
    shorter). No FIXME when it appends (CX logs one; 10.0_8 doesn't)
Upstream: no
Why: The Chromium (CEF) interfaces of Steam, Battle.net, the EA app, GOG
 Galaxy and Rockstar's Social Club render blank or crash in Wine unless
 their GPU process runs in-process with a software GL. When the program
 being created has one of these file names (in any case), kernelbase
 appends the switches for it (not to Steam's or GOG's crash reporter). A
 user's CommandLineAppend (kernelbase-commandline-append) comes after
 them, so a switch the user sets wins. It applies only to processes
 another Windows process creates (a launcher, start.exe, a shortcut), not
 to the one `wine <exe>` starts: start that with `wine start` instead.
Programs: Steam, Battle.net, EA app, GOG Galaxy, Rockstar Social Club: their
 CEF UIs render blank or crash
Matches: exe Battle.net.exe, EADesktop.exe, GalaxyClient.exe, GalaxyClient
 Helper.exe, GOG Galaxy Notifications Renderer.exe, steamwebhelper.exe,
 SocialClubHelper.exe (the started program's file name, any case); cmdline
 --type=crashpad-handler (the GOG Galaxy and steamwebhelper entries skip a
 command line containing it)
Risk: always on: changes the command line of the seven named programs,
 forcing software GL; --no-sandbox turns off steamwebhelper's renderer
 sandbox, which in Wine isolates nothing anyway (CX did the same)
Uncertain: whether any entry is still needed on Wine 11.x or with current
 launcher versions: each needs retesting with the launcher in a GUI
 session (review decision 14). Only the Steam and Big Fish entries can
 be tested here; the others are kept on their sources' word, untested
 (2026-11-26).
 CrossOver dropped the function after 24.x (26.3.0 has none); why isn't
 known. Sikarugir's reasons for the EA, GOG and Social Club flags aren't
 recorded.
Evidence: Sikarugir 10.0_8: kernelbase.dll (both arches): a 7-entry
 {exe_name, append, required_args, forbidden_args} table in .rdata (x86_64
 file offset 0x9c1a0) with exactly these strings and
 NULL/'--type=crashpad-handler' fields; no 'HACK: appending' string.
Expects before it: kernelbase-replace-launchers (context)
Related: ntdll-cef-command-line-args; it makes Epic's and Ubisoft's CEF
 honour command-line switches (this table has no entry for either)
Status: 10.0: 2026-11-26: matches the program's file name with wcsicmp
 instead of wcsstr on the path
 10.0: 2026-11-26: quick builds 20261126T013940 and T024442 (macOS 26.7.2,
 M1 Max): smoke and the regression suite passed

